        "# Q9) Information Available in a Discrete Random Variable\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1. The Probability Space\n",
        "*   **Sample Space ($\\Omega$):**\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\Omega=\\{G_1,G_2,D_1,D_2\\}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathfrak F=\\mathfrak P(\\Omega)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathfrak{F} = \\{\\emptyset, \\{G_1\\}, \\{G_2\\}, \\{D_1\\}, \\{D_2\\}, \\{G_1, G_2\\}, \\{G_1,D_1\\},\\{G_1,D_2\\}, \\{G_2,D_1\\},\\{G_2,D_2\\},\\{D_1,D_2\\},\\{G_1,G_2,D_1\\},\\{G_1,G_2,D_2\\},\\{G_1,D_1,D_2\\},\\{G_2,D_1,D_2\\}, \\Omega\\}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "### 2. $\\sigma$-algebra generated by $X$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$X^{-1}(0) = \\{G_1, G_2\\}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$X^{-1}(1) = \\{D_1, D_2\\}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "since,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$ \\sigma(X) = \\{\\emptyset, \\{G_1, G_2\\}, \\{D_1, D_2\\}, \\Omega\\} $$\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### 3. $\\sigma$-algebra generated by $Y$\n",
        "\n",
        "Since each outcome is assigned a distinct value, $Y$ distinguishes every point of $\\Omega$. Therefore\n",
        "\n",
        "$$ \\sigma(Y) = \\mathfrak{P}(\\Omega) = \\mathfrak{F} $$\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### 4. Subset Relation\n",
        "\n",
        "Every element in $\\sigma(X)$  is explicitly contained within $\\mathfrak{P}(\\Omega)$.\n",
        "\n",
        "but $$\n",
        "\\sigma(Y)=\\mathfrak P(\\Omega).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Therefore:\n",
        "$$ \\sigma(X) \\subseteq \\sigma(Y) $$\n",
        "\n",
        "$Y$ contains more information because knowing $Y$ reveals both the quality and the specific machine.\n",
        "\n",
        "Knowing $X$ reveals only the quality, grouping the machines together and leaving uncertainty.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### 5. Marginal Distribution of $X$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$ P(X=0) = P(\\{G_1, G_2\\}) = 0.50 + 0.20 = \\mathbf{0.70} $$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$ P(X=1) = P(\\{D_1, D_2\\}) = 0.10 + 0.20 = \\mathbf{0.30} $$\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### 6. Marginal Distribution of $Y$\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "$$ P(Y=1) = P(\\{G_1\\}) = \\mathbf{0.50} $$\n",
        "$$ P(Y=2) = P(\\{G_2\\}) = \\mathbf{0.20} $$\n",
        "$$ P(Y=3) = P(\\{D_1\\}) = \\mathbf{0.10} $$\n",
        "$$ P(Y=4) = P(\\{D_2\\}) = \\mathbf{0.20} $$\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### 7. Joint Probabilities $P(X, Y)$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$ P(X=0, Y=1) = P(\\{G_1, G_2\\} \\cap \\{G_1\\}) = P(\\{G_1\\}) = \\mathbf{0.50} $$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$ P(X=0, Y=2) = P(\\{G_1, G_2\\} \\cap \\{G_2\\}) = P(\\{G_2\\}) = \\mathbf{0.20} $$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$ P(X=1, Y=3) = P(\\{D_1, D_2\\} \\cap \\{D_1\\}) = P(\\{D_1\\}) = \\mathbf{0.10} $$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$ P(X=1, Y=4) = P(\\{D_1, D_2\\} \\cap \\{D_2\\}) = P(\\{D_2\\}) = \\mathbf{0.20} $$\n",
        "\n",
        "### 8. Conditional Probabilities\n",
        "\n",
        "$$ P(X=1 \\mid Y=3) = \\frac{P(X=1, Y=3)}{P(Y=3)} = \\frac{0.10}{0.10} = \\mathbf{1} $$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$ P(X=1 \\mid Y=4) = \\frac{P(X=1, Y=4)}{P(Y=4)} = \\frac{0.20}{0.20} = \\mathbf{1} $$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$ P(Y=3 \\mid X=1) = \\frac{P(X=1, Y=3)}{P(X=1)} = \\frac{0.10}{0.30} = \\mathbf{\\frac{1}{3}} $$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$ P(Y=4 \\mid X=1) = \\frac{P(X=1, Y=4)}{P(X=1)} = \\frac{0.20}{0.30} = \\mathbf{\\frac{2}{3}} $$\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### 9. Interpretation of Conditioning\n",
        "*   **Conditioning on $Y$:**\n",
        "\n",
        "Knowing $Y$ provides perfect information about $X$.\n",
        "If we know the exact item and machine , there is zero uncertainty about its quality .\n",
        "\n",
        "*   **Conditioning on $X$:**\n",
        "\n",
        "Knowing $X$ provides incomplete information. If we know an item is defective we still have uncertainty regarding which machine produced it\n",
        "\n",
        "### 10. Information differences in $\\sigma$-algebras\n",
        "*   **In $\\sigma(X)$ but not in $\\sigma(Y)$:**   Nothing\n",
